high triplet screening and automation  she has successfully developed an  embedded automation of several manual  processes  via miniaturization use of liquid  handling robots and scientific knowledge  but in her current role she is advancing  automation  and data capabilities to further support  streamlining of early biopharmaceutical  drug discovery  processes felicia the stage is yours  thank you nilis for the introduction and  for the opportunity to talk about  our uv vis protein quantification  workflow  using a liquid handler system which is  integrated with a lunatic  the agenda for my presentation today is  as follows i'm going to give a high  level overview of our workflow  the past and present state of why we  decided to proceed  with an integrated system how the liquid  handler and lunatic integrated system  performed  uv vis protein quantification and  finally  an example of our assay characterization  where we use the stutter for dls  for the overview of our workflow first  we receive a panel of purified proteins  which require screening  the liquid handler integrated system is  used to perform  all sample preparation a part of this  process  is to perform a uv-vis measurement via  the lunatic  to quantify the concentration of our  proteins  using the outputs from this measurement  the system will calculate the new  amount of sample and buffer required to  normalize the sample  and generate a new concentration new  concentrations are required throughout  our screen in order  to analyze our proteins by different  characterization assays  so where did we begin previously we  would need to perform all sample  preparation manually  using two different systems for uv vis  quantification  depending on the concentration we were  trying to achieve  one was used for concentration up to 20  mix to mill which was faster  and the other was up to 150 mg per ml  both systems were manual and it was  possible to measure one sample at a time  if there was a larger panel of protein  this was challenging and often required  repeats  due to failed readings or high  percentage cb  observed between replicates we wanted to  increase the speed at which we could  perform  our uv biz measurements that was a high  throughput  and low sample requirement this system  would ideally be  automation friendly such that the system  would be future proof  the lunatic was a product that looked  like it could fit this gap  in our process so we decided to  investigate it and compare it to our  current systems  during our comparison we identified that  the lunatic was comparable to both  of our systems up to their respective  concentration range  we decided to purchase the system and  investigate the possibility  to integrate on the liquid handler  system  what we managed to achieve was a system  which was fully automated  that could perform our uv-vis protein  concentration workflow  in the video you will see the liquid  handler  mixes the samples before aspirating and  dispensing  into the lunatic plates once this is  complete  the lunatic door will open and the  gripper arm  will transport the plates into the  lunatic  once the door is shut the measurement  will begin  and this will take some time depending  on the protocol  and the number of samples that we're  running  for today's purposes i've skipped the  wait time  once the measurement is complete the  door will open  and the gripper arm will retrieve the  plate it will then shut the door  and the workflow will continue using the  results from the lunatic  to generate new samples with a different  concentration  required for our assay characterization  for instance dls  for our assay characterization we  perform dynamic light scattering to  measure the size of our protein  in a solution previously we were  performing this manually  and the dlf assay required large amounts  of sample therefore we investigated  methods to miniaturize this assay  to reduce our overall sample requirement  we already knew the stutter plates were  avenable to automation  as we used the lunatic plates and our  protein concentration workflow  we compared the stunner to our legacy  dls system  which showed reproducible results where  the legacy system is shown in orange  and the stunner in the blue the shapes  are paired  are pairs of the same sample this showed  we could produce  the same data quality as our legacy data  using ninety percent less sample  and now we are routinely using the  scanners for our dls workflow  to summarize we have integrated the  lunatic with our liquid handler  this has allowed us to increase our  sample preparation efficiency  and speed reduce our personal resource  since we can walk away during sample  preparation and enhance the throughput  of our screen  for the standard we have reduced our  sample requirement by greater than 90  thank you for your attention i hope you  enjoyed this presentation  and i'll now hand back over to nellis  thank you all for attending our  presentation matt  felicia and myself will take any  questions now  you nellis matt and felicia for your  informative presentation  we will now begin the q a portion of the  webinar  if you have a question you would like to  ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  let's get started our first question is  so nellis this question will be for you  can this method be used with rnas  yes that's the short answer our or our  system has  applications for dna rna  any kinds of protein so this also works  with all the integration steps so yes  thank you nellis and i've got another  question coming your way  what's the concentration range of  lunatic  so the lunatic has a concentration range  of 0.03 od  to 275 od that translates  in for dna double stranded it would  translate into 1.5 nanograms per  microliter  up until 13.7 milligrams per microliter  and for protein about 0.03  uh mixed per ml to 275 mix per ml  thank you nellis matt this question is  for you  how many samples can you handle in a day  10 plates of 96  as shown on the slides was this possible  with your former method  sure so that's a great question um the  previous method we were closer to  two to five plates and that was a very  busy hands-on day  with the integration our average tends  to be about 10 plates which allows us to  get through the entire ngs workflow  in the same day um on heavy days you've  actually been able to do  15 plus plates um without any  slowdown the bottleneck tends to be more  of the extraction process  uh the lunatic is quick enough to keep  up with as much as we throw at it  all right thank you matt felicia this is  for you  how much is your screening throughput  enhanced with lunatic and you can give  me just a ballpark number so i would say  our screening throughput has been  haunted by at least  five folds by basic integration  all right that's amazing thank you and  looks like we have one more question for  matt and felicia  how difficult was it to actually  integrate the lunatic  um sure i could start it's actually very  simple  the device itself was designed to not  only look good but actually be very  integration friendly uh  the flat face of the device allows for a  lot of robot access  so you have a lot of options um as to  where you put it another thing like i  mentioned in the  slides was the the plate itself is svs  format  um so allows us to use any kind of  robotic arm storage  or liquid handlers um for the most part  if you can handle a 3d four-wheel plate  um i've found that you can handle the  wound with six plates with weights just  fine  i would agree with matt as well that the  integration was pretty easy i think  overall it probably took us  about a week plus um to integrate the  system and then  start having our protocols and workflows  in place um  we had a similar system before that we  had integrated  so the predecessor drops and i think  that made it a lot easier as well  um and then in terms of communication  everything like  a network problem so i guess that makes  it nicer in terms of being  wireless  all right thank you again matt and  felicia  and uh thank you to all of our speakers  nellis dennis  matt humes and felicia desarto joseph  for your time today and for your  important research  we would also like to thank labruz and  our sponsor unchained  labs for underwriting today's  educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today  and for their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speakers  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  roots will alert you via email when it's  available for replay we encourage you to  share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  we and we until next time excuse me  until next time  goodbye


